1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,975
The Cathay Building:
Singapore's First Skyscraper

2
00:00:03,975 --> 00:00:07,820
At the corner of Handy Road
and Dhoby Ghaut, a cream Art

3
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:11,535
Deco tower with the word
CATHAY running down its face

4
00:00:11,615 --> 00:00:15,400
still stands in front of a
glass-walled shopping mall.

5
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:18,998
Most people walking under it
are on their way to a movie

6
00:00:19,078 --> 00:00:22,675
or a meal and have no idea
that the mall behind it isn't

7
00:00:22,755 --> 00:00:26,090
the original building at
all — the tower is the only

8
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:29,899
piece of it left, and it's
technically a national monument

9
00:00:29,979 --> 00:00:33,525
even though the building it
belonged to was torn down.

10
00:00:33,525 --> 00:00:38,581
The building was privately financed,
not built by the colonial government:

11
00:00:38,661 --> 00:00:41,843
Dato Loke Wan Tho and
his mother put up roughly

12
00:00:41,923 --> 00:00:45,869
1 million Singapore dollars
of the family's tin and rubber

13
00:00:45,949 --> 00:00:50,172
fortune to build it, with
construction starting on the 11th of

14
00:00:50,252 --> 00:00:54,625
July 1939 to a design by the
British architect Frank W. Brewer.

15
00:00:54,625 --> 00:00:58,991
When the Cathay Cinema opened
that October, it was the tallest

16
00:00:59,071 --> 00:01:04,227
building in Singapore and in South-East
Asia, easily clearing the Supreme

17
00:01:04,307 --> 00:01:08,825
Court next door, and it stayed
the region's tallest until 1954.

18
00:01:08,825 --> 00:01:13,504
It was also the island's first fully
air-conditioned public building —

19
00:01:13,584 --> 00:01:16,699
armchair seating and cool
air were novel enough

20
00:01:16,779 --> 00:01:20,450
that people came just to
experience the cinema itself.

21
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:24,316
The complex grew to include
a hotel, serviced apartments,

22
00:01:24,396 --> 00:01:28,538
and a broadcasting studio, and
the Loke family kept a private

23
00:01:28,618 --> 00:01:32,425
penthouse on top with a view
over the Straits of Johor.

24
00:01:32,425 --> 00:01:36,815
Before the war reached Singapore,
the British military had already

25
00:01:36,895 --> 00:01:41,488
requisitioned much of the building
for its height, installing a radar

26
00:01:41,568 --> 00:01:45,957
station to coordinate air defence
between Singapore's airbases and

27
00:01:46,037 --> 00:01:50,575
housing the Far Eastern Bureau of
the Ministry of Economic Warfare.

28
00:01:50,575 --> 00:01:53,554
That height is exactly
what made the building

29
00:01:53,634 --> 00:01:57,100
useful to two different
armies in quick succession.

30
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:01,845
On the 15th of February 1942, as
Singapore's fall became unavoidable,

31
00:02:01,925 --> 00:02:05,760
the British were instructed
to fly a Japanese flag and a

32
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:10,235
white flag from the top of the
Cathay Building for ten minutes —

33
00:02:10,315 --> 00:02:15,000
a signal visible across the city,
from the tallest structure in it.

34
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,220
The Japanese renamed the
building Daitoa Gekijo ("Greater

35
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:23,295
East Asian Theatre") and ran
Radio Syonan's propaganda

36
00:02:23,375 --> 00:02:27,675
broadcasts from inside it for
the rest of the Occupation.

37
00:02:27,675 --> 00:02:31,658
In October 1943, Subhas Chandra
Bose used the building to

38
00:02:31,738 --> 00:02:35,222
proclaim the Provisional
Government of Free India,

39
00:02:35,302 --> 00:02:40,568
calling on Indians to "strike for
India's freedom" from the same tower that

40
00:02:40,648 --> 00:02:44,925
had signalled Singapore's
surrender eighteen months earlier.

41
00:02:44,925 --> 00:02:47,736
When Japan itself surrendered in 1945,

42
00:02:47,816 --> 00:02:53,212
the building changed hands one more
time: Admiral Lord Louis Mountbatten

43
00:02:53,292 --> 00:02:57,400
set up his South East Asia
Command headquarters there.

44
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:00,500
Civilian life came back slowly.

45
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:03,570
The Cathay Restaurant reopened in 1948,

46
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:09,225
the complex became a proper hotel
in 1954 — the same year the neon-lit

47
00:03:09,305 --> 00:03:13,264
nights above are from —
and stayed one until 1970,

48
00:03:13,344 --> 00:03:17,625
after which most of the
building turned into offices.

49
00:03:17,625 --> 00:03:21,055
A 1978 renovation covered over much of the

50
00:03:21,135 --> 00:03:25,151
original Art Deco detailing,
and a 1990 extension

51
00:03:25,231 --> 00:03:28,825
added an arthouse
cinema, The Picturehouse.

52
00:03:28,825 --> 00:03:33,168
By 1999 the Cathay Organisation
had decided the ageing

53
00:03:33,248 --> 00:03:38,001
complex needed a full rebuild
rather than another patch-up,

54
00:03:38,081 --> 00:03:42,750
and the original cinema closed
for the last time in 2000.

55
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:45,955
Demolition began in 2003
— and that same year,

56
00:03:46,035 --> 00:03:51,382
the building was gazetted a National
Monument, one of the first in Singapore

57
00:03:51,462 --> 00:03:55,382
to be protected under a new
scheme that preserves only a

58
00:03:55,462 --> 00:03:59,675
building's historic facade
rather than the whole structure.

59
00:03:59,675 --> 00:04:04,198
Everything behind that Art
Deco face on Handy Road today,

60
00:04:04,278 --> 00:04:07,509
including the condominium tower above it,

61
00:04:07,589 --> 00:04:11,950
is new construction that
opened as The Cathay in 2006.

62
00:04:11,950 --> 00:04:16,067
Why it matters today: most
Singaporeans have walked past that

63
00:04:16,147 --> 00:04:20,057
CATHAY sign without knowing
it once flew a surrender flag,

64
00:04:20,137 --> 00:04:24,460
broadcast Japanese propaganda,
and hosted the birth of a foreign

65
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:29,150
independence movement — all a decade
before it even became a hotel.

66
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:33,070
The tower's monument status
is itself an admission of how

67
00:04:33,150 --> 00:04:36,859
thin the line got: Singapore
didn't save the building,

68
00:04:36,939 --> 00:04:41,150
only its face, and decided that
was worth protecting anyway.
